C = [
("h1", "4. Jeremiah and Ezekiel: The Unfaithful Wife"),
("p", "Claim: Jeremiah and Ezekiel state the charge against the wife and then face the hardest question the figure raises. The law forbids a husband to take back a wife whom he sent away and who became another man’s. Yet the LORD, who knows that law, says to the wife, “return again to me.” The restoration is therefore the husband’s own act of grace toward the same wife, and it is made under an everlasting covenant. It is not a divorce followed by a second marriage to someone else."),
("sub", "4.1 The charge"),
("p", "Jeremiah sets the early love beside the later departure. The LORD remembers “the love of thine espousals” (Jeremiah 2:2) and says of Israel in that time, “Israel was holiness unto the LORD, and the firstfruits of his increase” (Jeremiah 2:3). Then comes the accusation in the language of marriage: “Surely as a wife treacherously departeth from her husband, so have ye dealt treacherously with me, O house of Israel, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 3:20). Ezekiel is told to “cause Jerusalem to know her abominations” (Ezekiel 16:2), and the charge in his parable is the same: “But as a wife that committeth adultery, which taketh strangers instead of her husband!” (Ezekiel 16:32). The wife is Jerusalem and the house of Israel, and her fault is that she took strangers in place of her husband."),
("sub", "4.2 The separation"),
("p", "Both prophets say that the husband acted. Jeremiah looks back on the northern kingdom: “for all the causes whereby backsliding Israel committed adultery I had put her away, and given her a bill of divorce; yet her treacherous sister Judah feared not, but went and played the harlot also” (Jeremiah 3:8). Both sisters are charged, and Judah is charged for learning nothing from Israel’s example. Ezekiel gives the sentence in the same terms: “I will judge thee, as women that break wedlock and shed blood are judged” (Ezekiel 16:38). The putting away is not a mistaken metaphor for a mild rebuke. It is a judgment with a name and a penalty."),
("sub", "4.3 The question of the law"),
("p", "The law of Moses sets a limit on divorce. A man who has sent his wife away may not take her again if she has been another man’s: “Her former husband, which sent her away, may not take her again to be his wife, after that she is defiled; for that is abomination before the LORD” (Deuteronomy 24:4). Jeremiah quotes the question that arises from that law, and he puts it in the mouth of the people: “They say, If a man put away his wife, and she go from him, and become another man’s, shall he return unto her again? shall not that land be greatly polluted?” (Jeremiah 3:1). The expected answer is no. Then the LORD gives his own answer, and it is the opposite of what the law would allow between men: “but thou hast played the harlot with many lovers; yet return again to me, saith the LORD.”"),
("p", "The whole book of the wife turns on that “yet.” The husband sets aside the pattern of the law by his own grace, and he does so without taking another wife. He says to the same children, “Turn, O backsliding children, saith the LORD; for I am married unto you: and I will take you one of a city, and two of a family, and I will bring you to Zion” (Jeremiah 3:14). In the days of the return, “the house of Judah shall walk with the house of Israel” (Jeremiah 3:18). Both houses that were charged are gathered in the one restoration."),
("tbl", "The table follows the charge, the separation, the question and the answer.", [
    ("Jer 3:20", "“Surely as a wife treacherously departeth from her husband, so have ye dealt treacherously with me”", "The charge is made in the language of marriage"),
    ("Jer 3:8", "“I had put her away, and given her a bill of divorce”", "The husband put the wife away"),
    ("Deut 24:4", "“Her former husband, which sent her away, may not take her again to be his wife”", "The law forbids the return between men"),
    ("Jer 3:1", "“yet return again to me, saith the LORD”", "The LORD calls the wife back in spite of the law’s pattern"),
    ("Jer 3:14", "“Turn, O backsliding children, saith the LORD; for I am married unto you”", "The call is to the same children, and no other wife is named"),
    ("Jer 3:18", "“the house of Judah shall walk with the house of Israel”", "Both houses are gathered"),
    ("Ezek 16:32", "“a wife that committeth adultery, which taketh strangers instead of her husband”", "The wife’s fault is the taking of strangers"),
    ("Ezek 16:60", "“I will remember my covenant with thee in the days of thy youth, and I will establish unto thee an everlasting covenant”", "The old covenant is remembered and an everlasting one is set up"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "4.4 The everlasting covenant"),
("p", "Ezekiel closes his parable with a promise that repeats the movement of Hosea. After the judgment, “I will remember my covenant with thee in the days of thy youth, and I will establish unto thee an everlasting covenant” (Ezekiel 16:60). The covenant that is remembered is the covenant of the espousals, and the covenant that is established is made with the same woman. The purpose is stated in the closing verses: “And I will establish my covenant with thee; and thou shalt know that I am the LORD” (Ezekiel 16:62). The knowledge of the LORD that Hosea made the last word of the betrothal (Hosea 2:20) is the last word here also. The restored wife is a wife who knows the one who took her back."),
("sub", "4.5 The marriage is a covenant"),
("p", "The prophets do not describe the marriage as a feeling. They describe it as a covenant. In Ezekiel the LORD says of the foundling, “I sware unto thee, and entered into a covenant with thee” (Ezekiel 16:8), and when the wife breaks it he charges her with having “despised the oath in breaking the covenant” (Ezekiel 16:59). The restoration is also a covenant, “an everlasting covenant” (Ezekiel 16:60). Hosea’s promise of the betrothal comes in the same passage as a covenant: “in that day will I make a covenant for them” (Hosea 2:18), and then, “I will betroth thee unto me for ever” (Hosea 2:19)."),
("p", "The most direct statement is in Jeremiah 31, the chapter of the new covenant. “Behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel, and with the house of Judah” (Jeremiah 31:31). The covenant that is to be replaced is the one that “they brake, although I was an husband unto them” (Jeremiah 31:32). The husband’s title is stated in the middle of the covenant promise. The two parties who are named, in this chapter and again when Hebrews quotes it (Hebrews 8:8, 10), are the house of Israel and the house of Judah."),
("p", "The argument is short. The marriage is a covenant, and the new covenant is made with Israel and Judah. A reader who holds that the Body is the wife must say that the Body is a party to a covenant that the text makes with Israel and Judah. The position of this volume reads the verses as they stand: the wife is the covenant people, and the covenant is named with her house. The question of how the Body stands toward the new covenant is taken up in its own volume in this series, and this volume asks only that the parties be read as the text names them."),
("sub", "4.6 The voice that is heard again"),
("p", "Jeremiah’s promise of the return carries the figure into the city itself. In the places that are desolate, in the streets of Jerusalem, there shall again be heard “the voice of joy, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride” (Jeremiah 33:11). The reason is given in the same verse: “For I will cause to return the captivity of the land, as at the first, saith the LORD.” The bride’s voice is heard in Jerusalem when the captivity of Israel and Judah returns (Jeremiah 33:7). It is a promise to the wife in her own city."),
("sub", "4.7 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that the law of Deuteronomy 24 is wrong. It says that the LORD, who gave the law, grants the return by an act of his own that the law does not grant to a man.",
    "It does not say who the “sisters” of Ezekiel 16 are in every detail, or fix the date of the everlasting covenant. Those questions belong to the volume on the New Covenant.",
    "It does not say that the putting away was final. The prophets say the opposite, and they say it in the same breath as the charge.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "The wife who departed is Jerusalem, the house of Israel and Judah. The husband put her away, and he called her back by grace against the pattern of the law. He promises an everlasting covenant with her, and he names no other wife."),
]
